Z||000000||Art Doyle||11-13-2001||08:53 PM||||I'd like to add a more modern control unit...hopefully with a knock sensor and aggressive advance.<P>Any leads or tips are much appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||MR2KLR||11-13-2001||10:11 PM||||do a search for Cyclone/ Typhoon or 2.0 turbo Sunbird. I think that is what the turbo 60° v6 guys use. I have also hear from "Rockcrawl" that the 90-92 TPI F-body ECM is programable.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mickey_Moose||11-13-2001||10:27 PM||||You could always try a haltech programmable computer - a fellow in our club is giving it a try...<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000003||terryk||11-13-2001||10:28 PM||||Any GM ECM is "programmable" meaning the eprom contents can be changed (but not on the fly).<P>There's no real reason to change the ECM on these engines unless you are doing something extraodinary to them like boost. Even then the V6 can already handle that. <P>Is there a particular reason you want to change?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Art Doyle||11-20-2001||11:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>Any GM ECM is "programmable" meaning the eprom contents can be changed (but not on the fly).<P>There's no real reason to change the ECM on these engines unless you are doing something extraodinary to them like boost. Even then the V6 can already handle that. <P><BR>Better fuel economy, slightly more power. I'm thinking that these ECU's are limited by their lack of a knock sensor and full closed loop operation of the oxygen sensor.<P>Is there a particular reason you want to change?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||11-21-2001||12:32 AM||||Not really. 7748 is a nice ECM.||||reg||1
